# Monthly restore drill over the newest D1 backups (fleet-ops#10594).
#
# What it proves: an encrypted dump that sits in a GitHub artifact really can be
# turned back into a database, on the host that already holds the private key,
# without a Cloudflare token and without creating anything in Cloudflare.
# fleet-ops#10594 asked for exactly this, because a backup nobody has restored
# is a claim, not a backup.
#
# What it does, in four steps and no script:
#   1. Walks the newest completed runs of drive's weekly d1-export.yml and takes
#      the first that left a d1-backup-* artifact, and writes the one date. That
#      date is written once here (IST) and every later step reads it, so a
#      catch-up run that crosses midnight still names the report with one string.
#   2. Downloads every d1-backup-* artifact of that run with gh, as the
#      nishfleet-worker App with a one-hour Actions-write token for that one repo
#      (fleet-worker-github-token@actions:drive.service, the same unit
#      fleet-pr-sweep@drive.service uses, fleet-ops#10601). Actions write is
#      what carries actions read; the App has no narrower grant, and the token is
#      revoked and its env file deleted the moment the run ends, so a detached
#      worker on the same uid never finds a live one in the runtime dir between
#      drills.
#   3. Decrypts each dump with age and the private key at
#      ~/.config/drive/d1-backup-key.txt, loads the SQL into a fresh local
#      SQLite file with sqlite3 in safe mode, runs PRAGMA integrity_check, and
#      counts every table's rows.
#   4. Writes one report per drill to ~/workspaces/agent-state/d1-restore-drill/.
#
# What it deliberately does not do: it never calls the Cloudflare API. The
# restore target is a local SQLite file, so the drill cannot create, import
# into or delete a D1 database, and it needs no D1 token at all. That is the
# point of putting it here: the private key lives on this host and on no
# runner, and a GitHub Actions job could not reach it without a secret that
# Nish has ruled out (fleet-ops#10594).
#
# The dumps are real customer data (payment rows, email signups). They are
# decrypted only inside a 0700 runtime directory under /run/user (tmpfs), and
# systemd removes that directory when the unit stops, so nothing decrypted
# outlives the drill. The report carries counts and integrity results, never a
# row, a name or an email address.
#
# A database that has no working backup shows up as a missing artifact, not as a
# silent pass: step 1 fails when none of the newest completed runs left a
# d1-backup-* artifact, and the report lists only what was actually restored.
# Today tinystudio_email_signups has no artifact because its export answers HTTP
# 404 (fleet-ops#10830) and the drive database has none because the D1 export
# API refuses FTS5 virtual tables (drive#1027); both stay visible in the run's
# own job list instead of being hidden here.
#
# Gates, each a failed unit someone can list (OnFailure= pages): a completed run
# with a dump exists, each download succeeds, each decrypt succeeds, each load
# succeeds, each integrity_check answers ok, at least one database was restored,
# and the report exists. A dump that decrypts but does not load fails the unit,
# because that is the failure this drill exists to catch.
#
# %%, $$, and no printf escapes. systemd expands % and $ itself before any shell
# runs, so every shell $ is written $$ and every shell % is written %%
# (ci.yml gate fleet-ops#8382), and systemd also unescapes a backslash pair
# before the shell sees it. echo carries the newlines instead of printf, so the
# only backslashes in this file are the pairs a quoted SQL identifier needs,
# written \\" the way fleet-grafana-github-token.service writes them. gh is
# found through /usr/bin/env, so the unit names no home path.
#
# Switch off: systemctl --user disable --now fleet-d1-restore-drill.timer, then
# drop step 2l and its enable line from fleet-sync.service and delete both files.

[Unit]
Description=Monthly restore drill over the newest D1 backups (fleet-ops#10594)
OnFailure=fleet-unit-failed@%N.service
Requires=fleet-worker-github-token@actions:drive.service
After=fleet-worker-github-token@actions:drive.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
RuntimeDirectory=fleet-d1-restore-drill
RuntimeDirectoryMode=0700
Environment=GH_REPO=Nishfleet/drive
Environment=GH_WORKFLOW=d1-export.yml
Environment=DRILL_KEY=%h/.config/drive/d1-backup-key.txt
Environment=DRILL_STATE=%h/workspaces/agent-state/d1-restore-drill
Environment=TZ=Asia/Kolkata
Environment=LC_ALL=C.UTF-8
EnvironmentFile=%t/fleet-worker-github-token/actions:drive.env
# The required EnvironmentFile above is loaded for every command below, so an
# empty token means a failed mint and the run must not fall back to the owner's
# gh login. $$ keeps systemd from expanding the token into the shell's argv.
ExecStartPre=/bin/sh -c 'test -n "$$GH_TOKEN" || { echo "drill: no GitHub App token, refusing to run" >&2; exit 1; }'
# 1. The newest completed run that actually left a dump, and the one date. The
#    walk takes the first run with a d1-backup-* artifact, because the thing the
#    drill restores is the newest backup, not the newest run: a run with no
#    artifact is not a backup. A branch filter was tried and is wrong here,
#    because drive's own main matrix still fails on purpose (drive#1027, the
#    export API refuses a database with FTS5 virtual tables) and the product rows
#    sit on a branch until #10829 and #10830 land, so -b main would leave the
#    drill with nothing to restore every month. What a bad dump could do is not
#    choose a branch, it is run a dot-command: the recipient is a public key, so
#    anyone can encrypt a payload that decrypts cleanly, and step 3 therefore
#    loads it with sqlite3 in safe mode, which refuses .shell, .system, ATTACH
#    and every file-writing dot-command. Both gh calls are plain assignments, so
#    an expired token or a rate limit fails the unit instead of looking like a
#    run with no artifact. Eight runs is two months of the weekly schedule, which
#    is how far back a broken weekly export can be before the drill fails loud.
ExecStart=/bin/sh -c 'set -eu; umask 077; out="$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY"; \
  d=$$(date +%%F); echo "$$d" > "$$out/date"; \
  test -s "$$DRILL_KEY" || { echo "drill: the private key file is missing or empty" >&2; exit 1; }; \
  runs=$$(gh run list -R $$GH_REPO -w $$GH_WORKFLOW -s completed -L 8 --json databaseId -q ".[].databaseId"); \
  run=""; \
  for r in $$runs; do \
    names=$$(gh api "repos/$$GH_REPO/actions/runs/$$r/artifacts?per_page=100" --jq ".artifacts[].name"); \
    if echo "$$names" | grep -q "^d1-backup-"; then run=$$r; break; fi; \
  done; \
  test -n "$$run" || { echo "drill: none of the newest completed $$GH_WORKFLOW runs in $$GH_REPO left a d1-backup-* artifact" >&2; exit 1; }; \
  echo "$$run" > "$$out/run"; \
  echo "drill: backup run $$run on $$d"'
# 2. Every d1-backup-* artifact of that run, in one call, with the one-hour
#    Actions-write token the token unit's Actions-only instance mints for drive.
#    gh run download takes the pattern and lands each artifact under
#    dumps/<artifact name>/, which is the layout step 3 reads, and it fails loud
#    when the pattern matches nothing: a run whose artifacts were pruned is a
#    failed drill and not an empty report.
ExecStart=/bin/sh -c 'set -eu; umask 077; out="$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY"; run=$$(cat "$$out/run"); \
  /usr/bin/env gh run download "$$run" -R $$GH_REPO -p "d1-backup-*" -D "$$out/dumps" \
  && echo "drill: downloaded the d1-backup-* artifacts of run $$run"'
# 3. Decrypt, load into a fresh local SQLite file, integrity-check, count rows.
#    The D1 export API hands back SQL text, not a database file, so the restore
#    is a load into an empty database: that is the step a backup claim skips.
#    Safe mode is the load-bearing guard: the recipient is a public key, so a
#    d1-backup-* artifact can be written by anyone who can run that workflow,
#    and without -safe the CLI would run .shell and .system from the dump on
#    this host as nish, with the private key and every credential readable.
#    Safe mode also refuses load_extension() and VACUUM INTO, both verified on
#    this host's sqlite3 3.45.1, so a dump cannot execute code or leave a file
#    behind. -bail stops the load at the first statement that fails, because a
#    dump that loads partly can still answer integrity_check ok.
#    The table names are read one per line and never word-split, so a name with
#    a space still counts, and the SQL is double-quoted (type = \\"table\\")
#    because a systemd Exec line is itself single-quoted, so no shell single
#    quote can appear in it. sqlite_sequence is SQLite's own AUTOINCREMENT
#    bookkeeping, not application data, so it is not counted among the tables
#    the dump made.
ExecStart=/bin/sh -c 'set -eu; umask 077; out="$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY"; : > "$$out/rows"; n=0; \
  for f in "$$out"/dumps/*/*.sql.age; do \
    test -e "$$f" || { echo "drill: no dump found under $$out/dumps" >&2; exit 1; }; \
    a=$$(basename "$$(dirname "$$f")"); b=$$(basename "$$f" .sql.age); db="$$out/$$b.db"; \
    age -d -i "$$DRILL_KEY" -o "$$out/$$b.sql" "$$f"; \
    rm -f "$$db"; sqlite3 -safe -bail "$$db" < "$$out/$$b.sql"; \
    ic=$$(sqlite3 -safe "$$db" "PRAGMA integrity_check;"); \
    test "$$ic" = ok || { echo "drill: $$a did not restore: integrity_check answered $$ic" >&2; exit 1; }; \
    sqlite3 -safe "$$db" "select name from sqlite_master where type = \\"table\\" and name not like \\"sqlite_%%\\" order by name" > "$$out/tn"; \
    : > "$$out/tbl"; tot=0; \
    while IFS= read -r t; do \
      c=$$(sqlite3 -safe "$$db" "select count(*) from \\"$$t\\""); tot=$$((tot+c)); echo "$$t=$$c" >> "$$out/tbl"; \
    done < "$$out/tn"; \
    det=$$(paste -sd" " "$$out/tbl"); \
    echo "| $$b | $$a | $$(wc -c < "$$out/$$b.sql") | ok | $$tot |" >> "$$out/rows"; \
    echo "- $$b: $$det" >> "$$out/tables"; \
    n=$$((n+1)); echo "drill: restored $$a from $$b, $$tot rows, integrity ok"; \
  done; \
  test "$$n" -gt 0 || { echo "drill: nothing was restored" >&2; exit 1; }'
# 4. One report per drill, in agent-state, which is private and never reaches a
#    public repo. Counts and integrity results only: no row, no name, no email
#    address. The report is named from the date file and not a fresh `date`, so a
#    run that crosses midnight still reads its own report.
ExecStart=/bin/sh -c 'set -eu; umask 077; out="$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY"; d=$$(cat "$$out/date"); run=$$(cat "$$out/run"); \
  mkdir -p "$$DRILL_STATE"; rep="$$DRILL_STATE/report-$$d.md"; \
  { echo "# D1 restore drill, $$d (Asia/Kolkata)"; echo; \
    echo "Backup run: https://github.com/$$GH_REPO/actions/runs/$$run"; \
    echo "Source: the weekly $$GH_WORKFLOW matrix in $$GH_REPO (fleet-ops#10594)."; \
    echo "Key: the host-held private key (path in this unit)"; \
    echo "Where: this host, into a 0700 runtime directory that systemd removes when the unit ends. No Cloudflare database was created and no Cloudflare API call was made."; echo; \
    echo "| database | artifact | dump bytes | integrity | rows |"; echo "|---|---|---|---|---|"; \
    cat "$$out/rows"; echo; echo "Per table:"; cat "$$out/tables"; echo; \
    echo "$$(wc -l < "$$out/rows") database(s) restored."; \
  } > "$$rep"; \
  echo "drill: report $$rep"'
# One ExecStopPost, and the revoke is its first command: systemd loads
# EnvironmentFile= again for every command it spawns, so a revoke after the rm
# could not start, and it stops at the first command that fails, so anything
# ordered before this line would leave a live Actions-write token and its env
# file in the runtime dir whenever the drill fails. Every ExecStart step above
# already fails with its own message, so there is nothing left to diagnose here.
# The revoke runs only with the App token loaded (an empty GH_TOKEN would fall
# back to the owner's login), and a failed revoke still fails the unit.
ExecStopPost=/bin/sh -c 'rc=0; [ -z "$$GH_TOKEN" ] || /usr/bin/env gh api -X DELETE installation/token || rc=$$?; rm -f %t/fleet-worker-github-token/actions:drive.env; exit $$rc'
TimeoutStartSec=1800
